WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.610
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:11.150
Today we're going to talk about an algorithm
that is capable of filling holes in space

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:12.150
and time.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:17.650
Classical image inpainting, or in other words,
filling holes in images is something that

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.980
we've explored in earlier episodes - there
are several incredible techniques to take

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:22.980
care of that.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.920
But with this piece of work, it is possible
to generalize such a technique for video,

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.200
and fill holes for not only one image, but
a series of images.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.500
Like removing a umbrella to create an unoccluded
view to the beach.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.160
Or removing a waving person from a video of
us jogging.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.280
These results are truly incredible, and even
though this method was published long long

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.920
ago, it still enjoys a great deal of reverence
among computer graphics practitioners.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.739
Not only that, but this algorithm also serves
the basis of the awesome content aware fill

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:59.920
feature introduced in Adobe Photoshop CS5.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.400
In this problem formulation, we have to make
sure that our solution has spatio-temporal

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.400
consistency.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.750
What does this mean exactly?

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.180
This means that holes can exist through space
and time, so multiple frames of a video may

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.580
be missing, or there may be regions that we
wish to cut out not only for one image, but

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.420
for the entirety of the video.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.190
The filled in regions have to be consistent
with their surroundings if they are looked

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:29.470
at as an image, but there also has to be a
consistency across the time domain, otherwise

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.780
we would see a disturbing flickering effect
in the results.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.689
It is a really challenging problem indeed
because there are more constraints that we

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:43.270
have to adhere to, however, a key observation
is that it is also easier because in return,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.940
we have access to more information that comes
from the previous and the next frames in the

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.940
video.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:54.060
For instance, here, you can see an example
of retouching old footage by removing a huge

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.330
pesky artifact.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:00.079
And clearly, we know the fact that Charlie
Chaplin is supposed to be in the middle of

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:04.810
the image only because we have this information
from the previous and next frames.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.170
All this is achieved by an optimization algorithm
that takes into consideration that consistency

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.470
has to be enforced through the spatial and
the time domain at the same time.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.420
It can also be used to fill in completely
missing frames of a video.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:25.060
Or, it also helps where we have parts of an
image missing after being removed by an image

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.970
stabilizer algorithm.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.060
Video editors do this all the time, so such
a restoration technique is super useful.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.590
The source code of this technique is available,
I've put a link to it in the video description.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

